{"id":"05116808b440eb9f7bbbafef65d21f244017eca29562d910fc117443c29feed2","lamport":0,"author":"scual.project@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-10-04T12:18:03.226616Z","format_version":1,"kind":"create","payload":{"fields":{"created-at":"2026-10-04T12:18:03.226616Z","created-by":"carlo.cifarelli@gmail.com","description":"Step 6/6 of the op-log roadmap (parent 0e7b1245), deferred.\n\nHigh-effort bidirectional bridge sync to external trackers (GitHub, GitLab, …),\nmirroring git-bug's bridge pattern. Non-critical to the distributed-tracker story;\nscheduled last.","parent":"0e7b1245-e860-4843-9df3-101cd9a0a746","priority":"5","spec":"# Spec — Bidirectional bridges (GitHub/GitLab)\n\nRoadmap step 6/6 (parent 0e7b12). Depends on step 4 (op-log). High effort, DEFERRED.\n\n## Goal\n\nSync tickets bidirectionally with external trackers (GitHub Issues, GitLab, …),\nmirroring git-bug's bridge pattern, so teams straddling ticgit and a hosted\ntracker keep a single source of truth in git.\n\n## Current state\n\n- ticgit already has one-directional import: `ti import gh|linear` (see\n  `reference.md`). This step generalizes to continuous, bidirectional sync.\n- The op-log (step 4) is the enabler: external changes map to ops, so merges with\n  local edits are conflict-free and re-import is idempotent.\n\n## Design\n\n- Bridge config per remote tracker: target platform, URL, auth token, mapping\n  rules. Follow git-bug's `bridge new --target=<platform> --url=... --token=...`\n  shape for familiarity. Store config locally (tokens NEVER in synced git-meta).\n- Import direction: external issue/comment → ticgit op (reuse step 4 op kinds).\n  Keep a stable external-id ↔ ticket-uuid mapping (in `meta:` fields or a bridge\n  namespace) so repeated syncs dedupe instead of duplicating.\n- Export direction: local ops → external API calls, with a watermark (last-synced\n  op head) per bridge so only new ops are pushed.\n- Conflict handling: because the op-log is the truth, a round-trip is a merge of\n  op sets, not a destructive overwrite; field-level divergence resolves by the\n  same (lamport, hash) rule as local merges.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `bridge` module + `ti bridge` command family (new/list/pull/push/configure).\n- Per-platform adapter trait; implement GitHub first, GitLab second.\n- Auth/token storage local-only; rate-limit + pagination handling per platform.\n\n## Acceptance (when undeferred)\n\n- Round-trip test against a recorded/mock GitHub API: external issue imports as a\n  ticket; local edit exports back; re-sync is idempotent (no duplicates, no\n  clobber).\n\n## Why deferred\n\n- Highest effort, external-API surface, and not required for the core distributed-\n  tracker story. Scheduled last; revisit after steps 1–5 land and the op-log has\n  proven stable in real use.","state":"new","status":"open","title":"Bidirectional bridges (GitHub/GitLab)"}}}